window.VOCAB_CLASSROOM = [{"id":"genitiv","week":1,"added":"2026-11-06","level":"B1.1","title":"Genitiv","title_en":"The genitive case: whose is it?","summary":"Show who something belongs to (*das Büro des Chefs*) and use *wegen, trotz, während, statt* in writing.","purpose":["The genitive shows that something **belongs to** someone or something: *das Büro **des Chefs*** (the boss's office), *der Sitz **der Firma*** (the company's headquarters).","In everyday speech, people often say *von* + dative instead: *das Büro **vom Chef***. You still need the genitive for reading, emails, formal speech and the B1 exam, and after a few prepositions such as *wegen* (because of) and *trotz* (despite)."],"logic":["The genitive phrase comes **after** the noun it describes: *das Auto **meiner Schwester*** (literally: the car of my sister). English *'s* goes the other way round.","Only **names** go in front: *Annas Wohnung* (Anna's flat). There is no apostrophe.","The signal sits mainly in the **article**. Masculine and neuter take **des / eines**, and the noun adds **-s** or **-es**. Feminine and plural take **der**, and the noun stays unchanged.","So *der* now has a third job: masculine nominative, feminine dative and **feminine or plural genitive**. The position in the sentence tells you which.","The question word is **wessen?** (whose?). In speech, *wem gehört …?* (who does … belong to?) is more common."],"rules":[{"title":"Article words in the genitive","text":"Learn the four columns as a pattern: **des / des / der / der**. Possessives (*mein, dein, unser* …) and *kein* follow *ein*. The plural has no indefinite article, so you use *von* + dative instead (see Exceptions).","table":{"caption":"Genitive forms","head":["","masculine","neuter","feminine","plural"],"rows":[["definite","**des** Chef**s**","**des** Kind**es**","**der** Firma","**der** Kunden"],["indefinite","**eines** Chef**s**","**eines** Kind**es**","**einer** Firma","—"],["possessive","**meines** Chef**s**","**meines** Kind**es**","**meiner** Firma","**meiner** Kunden"],["negative","**keines** Chef**s**","**keines** Kind**es**","**keiner** Firma","**keiner** Kunden"]]},"examples":[{"de":"Das ist das Büro **des Chefs**.","en":"That is the boss's office."},{"de":"Die Mitarbeitenden **der Firma** arbeiten oft im Homeoffice.","en":"The company's employees often work from home."},{"de":"Die Wünsche **unserer Kunden** sind uns wichtig.","en":"Our customers' wishes are important to us."}],"source":"kasus"},{"title":"Masculine and neuter nouns add -s or -es","text":["Use **-es** after an s-sound (*-s, -ss, -z, -tz, -x*): *des Hauses, des Platzes, des Fusses*. Nouns ending in *-nis* double the s: *des Ergebnisses*.","Use **-s** after *-el, -en, -er, -chen, -ling* and after a vowel: *des Computers, des Mädchens, des Autos*.","Many one-syllable nouns allow both, and **-es** sounds more formal: *des Tages* (much more common than *des Tags*), *des Kindes* or *des Kinds*."],"examples":[{"de":"Am Ende **des Monats** bekomme ich meinen Lohn.","en":"I get my wages at the end of the month."},{"de":"Die Ergebnisse **des Projekts** sind sehr gut.","en":"The project's results are very good.","note":"*des Projektes* is also correct."},{"de":"Die Miete **des Hauses** ist gestiegen.","en":"The rent for the house has gone up."}],"source":"s-es"},{"title":"Weak masculine nouns take -n or -en, never -s","text":"A group of masculine nouns, almost all people or animals, end in **-(e)n** in every case except the nominative: *der Kollege → des Kollegen*. Typical endings are *-e* (*Kollege, Kunde, Junge*), *-ent* and *-ant* (*Student, Praktikant*), *-ist* (*Polizist*), plus *Mensch, Herr* (*des Herrn*) and *Nachbar*.","examples":[{"de":"Ich habe die Telefonnummer **des Kunden** nicht.","en":"I don't have the customer's phone number."},{"de":"Das ist der Laptop **meines Kollegen**.","en":"That is my colleague's laptop."}],"source":"weak"},{"title":"Names go in front and add -s","text":"Put the name before the noun and add **-s**, with no apostrophe: *Peters Büro*. If the name already ends in an s-sound (*-s, -ss, -z, -x*), write only an apostrophe: *Thomas' Auto*, *Max' Fahrrad*. The noun after the name takes no article.","examples":[{"de":"**Wessen** Jacke ist das? – Das ist **Marias** Jacke.","en":"Whose jacket is that? – It's Maria's jacket."},{"de":"**Thomas'** Präsentation war am besten.","en":"Thomas's presentation was the best."}],"source":"s-es"},{"title":"Prepositions with the genitive","text":"These prepositions take the genitive in writing: **wegen** (because of), **trotz** (despite), **während** (during), **statt** (instead of), **innerhalb** (within, inside) and **ausserhalb** (outside of).","examples":[{"de":"**Wegen einer Störung** fahren heute keine Züge nach Bern.","en":"Because of a fault, no trains are running to Bern today."},{"de":"**Trotz des schlechten Wetters** gehen wir am Wochenende wandern.","en":"Despite the bad weather, we're going hiking at the weekend.","note":"After an article, adjectives in the genitive end in **-en**: *des schlechten Wetters*."},{"de":"**Während der Sitzung** bitte keine Handys.","en":"No mobile phones during the meeting, please."},{"de":"**Statt eines Taxis** nehmen wir das Tram.","en":"Instead of a taxi, we'll take the tram."},{"de":"**Innerhalb einer Woche** bekommen Sie eine Antwort.","en":"You'll get an answer within a week."}],"source":"prep"}],"exceptions":[{"title":"wegen + dative in speech","text":["In speech, many people say *wegen **dem** Regen* instead of *wegen **des** Regens*. It is widespread and hardly counted as an error in speech, but in writing *wegen des Regens* is still far more common and is the safe choice. *Trotz* keeps the genitive more firmly.","With pronouns, the dative is normal: *wegen mir* (because of me). The old form *meinetwegen* now mostly means \"fine by me\"."],"examples":[{"de":"Wegen **dem** Regen sind wir zu Hause geblieben. (spoken)","en":"Because of the rain, we stayed at home."},{"de":"Wegen **des Regens** sind wir zu Hause geblieben. (written)","en":"Because of the rain, we stayed at home."},{"de":"Kommst du **wegen mir**? – Ja, **meinetwegen**.","en":"Are you coming because of me? – Yes, fine by me.","note":"Here *meinetwegen* means \"fine by me\"."}],"source":"wegen"},{"title":"Plural without an article: use von + dative","text":"A plural noun with no article or adjective shows no genitive at all, so German uses *von* + dative instead.","examples":[{"de":"die Meinung **von Experten**","en":"the opinion of experts"},{"de":"Die Preise **von Wohnungen** in Zürich sind sehr hoch.","en":"The prices of flats in Zurich are very high."}],"source":"wegen"},{"title":"Some nouns add -ns","text":"A few masculine nouns ending in *-e* add **-ns** in the genitive: *der Name → des Namens*, *der Gedanke → des Gedankens*, *der Glaube → des Glaubens*.","examples":[{"de":"Die Schreibweise **des Namens** ist falsch.","en":"The spelling of the name is wrong."}],"source":"weak"},{"title":"Some foreign words and abbreviations take no ending","text":"Foreign words with an unstressed ending in *-us* or *-as* usually stay unchanged: *des Zirkus, des Atlas*. Abbreviations can go without an ending: *des LKW* or *des LKWs*. Never use an apostrophe for a missing ending: *des Atlas'* is wrong.","examples":[{"de":"die Fahrerin **des LKW**","en":"the lorry's driver"}],"source":"s-es"},{"title":"Feminine nouns never take an ending","text":"In the genitive only the article changes for feminine nouns: *die Firma → der Firma*, never *der Firmas*.","examples":[{"de":"die Adresse **der Bank**","en":"the bank's address"}],"source":"kasus"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"das Büro der Chef","right":"das Büro **des Chefs**","why":"Masculine: *des* plus *-s* on the noun."},{"wrong":"Anna's Wohnung","right":"**Annas** Wohnung","why":"German has no apostrophe before the *-s*."},{"wrong":"des Kollegens","right":"des **Kollegen**","why":"Weak nouns take *-n*, never *-s*."},{"wrong":"der Firmas","right":"der **Firma**","why":"Feminine nouns never take a genitive ending."},{"wrong":"mein Bruders Auto","right":"das Auto **meines Bruders**","why":"Only names go before the noun. Everything else follows it."},{"wrong":"trotz dem Regen (in an email)","right":"trotz **des Regens**","why":"In writing, use the genitive after *trotz* and *wegen*."}],"examples":[{"de":"Der Sitz **der Firma** ist in Zürich.","en":"The company's headquarters is in Zurich."},{"de":"Kennst du die Freundin **meines Bruders**?","en":"Do you know my brother's girlfriend?"},{"de":"Die Farbe **des Autos** gefällt mir nicht.","en":"I don't like the colour of the car."},{"de":"**Wegen der Baustelle** brauche ich zehn Minuten länger.","en":"Because of the roadworks, I need ten minutes longer."},{"de":"**Während der Ferien** ist das Büro geschlossen.","en":"The office is closed during the holidays."},{"de":"Der Name **des Hotels** ist schwierig.","en":"The hotel's name is difficult."},{"de":"Wir wohnen **ausserhalb der Stadt**.","en":"We live outside the city."},{"de":"Das ist der Hund **unserer Nachbarn**.","en":"That is our neighbours' dog."}],"later":"The genitive with adjectives in full (*trotz starken Regens*, *wegen guter Arbeit*) comes with the topic on adjective endings.","selfcheck":{"types":{"fill":{"label":"Fill the gap","instruction":"Put the words in brackets into the genitive. Write the whole phrase: article word plus noun.","example":{"q":"Das ist das Handy ___ (mein Vater).","a":"meines Vaters","why":"Masculine: *meines* plus *-s* on the noun."}},"rewrite":{"label":"Spoken → written","instruction":"Rewrite the spoken phrase with *von* or the dative in the written genitive.","example":{"q":"die Tür von dem Zimmer","a":"die Tür des Zimmers","why":"Neuter: *des* plus *-s*."}}},"items":[{"type":"fill","q":"Die Adresse ___ (die Schule) steht auf der Website.","a":["der Schule"],"why":"Feminine: *der*, with no ending on the noun."},{"type":"fill","q":"Das ist der Schreibtisch ___ (unser Praktikant).","a":["unseres Praktikanten"],"why":"*Praktikant* is a weak noun, so it takes *-en*, not *-s*."},{"type":"fill","q":"___ (wegen / der Regen) bleiben wir heute zu Hause.","a":["Wegen des Regens"],"why":"*wegen* + genitive in writing. *der Regen* is masculine: *des Regens*. In speech you'd also hear *wegen dem Regen*."},{"type":"fill","q":"Ich lese den Bericht ___ (das Team).","a":["des Teams"],"why":"Neuter: *des* plus *-s*."},{"type":"rewrite","q":"die Meinung von meiner Chefin","a":["die Meinung meiner Chefin"],"why":"Feminine possessive: *meiner*."},{"type":"rewrite","q":"während dem Essen","a":["während des Essens"],"why":"*während* takes the genitive in writing. *das Essen* is neuter: *des Essens*."}]},"sources":[{"id":"kasus","title":"grammis: Kasus (case)","url":"https://grammis.ids-mannheim.de/vggf/2218"},{"id":"s-es","title":"grammis: Eines Tags oder eines Tages? Genitive forms short and long","url":"https://grammis.ids-mannheim.de/fragen/26"},{"id":"weak","title":"grammis: Autor, Doktor, Friede, Funke (weak and mixed nouns)","url":"https://grammis.ids-mannheim.de/fragen/28"},{"id":"prep","title":"grammis: Präpositionalphrase (prepositions and the cases they take)","url":"https://grammis.ids-mannheim.de/kontrastive-grammatik/5747"},{"id":"wegen","title":"grammis: Wegen dem Regen oder wegen des Regens? Dative or genitive","url":"https://grammis.ids-mannheim.de/fragen/67"}]},{"id":"konjunktiv-2","week":2,"added":"2026-11-06","level":"B1.1","title":"Konjunktiv II","title_en":"The subjunctive: polite, wishful and unreal","summary":"Be polite (*Könnten Sie …?*), give advice (*Du solltest …*), make wishes and talk about what isn't real (*Wenn ich Zeit hätte, würde ich …*).","purpose":["The normal verb forms (the indicative) describe what **is** real: *Ich habe Zeit.* (I have time.) The Konjunktiv II describes what is **not** real, or not yet real: *Wenn ich Zeit **hätte**, …* (If I had time, …).","You also use it every day to sound **polite** and **less direct**: *Ich **hätte** gern einen Kaffee.* (I'd like a coffee.) *Könnten Sie mir helfen?* (Could you help me?) It roughly matches English *would*, *could* and *should*."],"logic":["Most verbs use **würde + infinitive**, the same pattern as a modal verb: *würde* in position 2, the infinitive at the end. *Ich **würde** gern in Bern **wohnen**.*","A few very common verbs have their **own one-word form**, built from the Präteritum with an umlaut: *war → **wäre***, *hatte → **hätte***, *konnte → **könnte***. Use these instead of *würde … sein / haben*.","The endings are the same for all of them: *ich -e, du -est, er/sie/es -e, wir -en, ihr -et, sie/Sie -en*.","For the **past**, use *hätte* or *wäre* plus the Partizip II, as in the Perfekt: *Ich **hätte** dich **angerufen**.* (I would have called you.)"],"rules":[{"title":"The one-word forms: wäre, hätte, würde, könnte","text":"Take the Präteritum (*war, hatte, wurde, konnte*), add an umlaut, and use the *-e* endings.","table":{"caption":"Konjunktiv II of the four key verbs","head":["","sein","haben","werden","können"],"rows":[["ich","**wäre**","**hätte**","**würde**","**könnte**"],["du","wär(e)st","hättest","würdest","könntest"],["er / sie / es","wäre","hätte","würde","könnte"],["wir","wären","hätten","würden","könnten"],["ihr","wär(e)t","hättet","würdet","könntet"],["sie / Sie","wären","hätten","würden","könnten"]]},"examples":[{"de":"Ich **wäre** jetzt gern in den Bergen.","en":"I'd like to be in the mountains right now."},{"de":"Wir **hätten** gern mehr Ferien.","en":"We'd like more holidays."}],"source":"k2"},{"title":"würde + infinitive for most other verbs","text":"*würde* goes in position 2 and the infinitive goes to the end, the same verb bracket as with *können* or *müssen*.","examples":[{"de":"Ich **würde** gern mehr **reisen**.","en":"I'd like to travel more."},{"de":"An deiner Stelle **würde** ich mit der Chefin **sprechen**.","en":"If I were you, I'd talk to the boss."}],"source":"flexion"},{"title":"Modal verbs: könnte, müsste, dürfte, möchte","text":"Modal verbs take an umlaut in the Konjunktiv II: *könnte* (could), *müsste* (would have to), *dürfte* (might, would be allowed to), *möchte* (would like). **Except** *sollte* (should) and *wollte* (would want): they have no umlaut, so they look the same as the Präteritum.","examples":[{"de":"**Dürfte** ich kurz stören?","en":"Might I interrupt for a moment?"},{"de":"Du **solltest** mehr Pausen machen.","en":"You should take more breaks."},{"de":"Eigentlich **müsste** ich heute noch einkaufen.","en":"Actually, I ought to go shopping today."}],"source":"modal"},{"title":"Four uses: polite, advice, wishes, unreal conditions","text":["**Polite requests:** *Könnten Sie …? Würden Sie …? Hätten Sie …? Ich hätte gern …*","**Advice:** *Du solltest … An deiner Stelle würde ich …*","**Wishes:** *Ich wäre gern … Ich hätte gern … Wenn ich doch mehr Zeit hätte!*","**Unreal conditions:** *Wenn ich mehr Geld hätte, würde ich weniger arbeiten.*"],"examples":[{"de":"**Könnten** Sie mir bitte die Rechnung schicken?","en":"Could you send me the invoice, please?"},{"de":"**Würden** Sie das bitte noch einmal erklären?","en":"Would you explain that again, please?"},{"de":"Wenn ich doch mehr Zeit **hätte**!","en":"If only I had more time!"}],"source":"k2"},{"title":"wenn-sentences: verb at the end, then verb first","text":"In the *wenn*-clause the conjugated verb goes to the **end**. If the *wenn*-clause comes first, the main clause **starts with the verb**: *Wenn ich Zeit hätte, **würde** ich kommen.*","examples":[{"de":"Wenn ich mehr Zeit **hätte**, **würde** ich einen Abendkurs **besuchen**.","en":"If I had more time, I'd take an evening class."},{"de":"Ich **würde** mitkommen, wenn ich nicht arbeiten **müsste**.","en":"I'd come along if I didn't have to work."}],"source":"flexion"},{"title":"The past: hätte / wäre + Partizip II","text":"To talk about something in the past that didn't happen, use *hätte* or *wäre* plus the Partizip II. The choice between *hätte* and *wäre* is the same as in the Perfekt (*haben* or *sein*).","examples":[{"de":"Wenn der Zug pünktlich **gewesen wäre**, **hätte** ich das Meeting nicht **verpasst**.","en":"If the train had been on time, I wouldn't have missed the meeting."},{"de":"Das **hätte** ich nicht **gedacht**.","en":"I wouldn't have thought that."}],"source":"k2"}],"exceptions":[{"title":"Regular verbs always use würde","text":"For regular (weak) verbs, the one-word Konjunktiv II looks exactly like the Präteritum: *ich arbeitete* can mean \"I worked\" or \"I would work\". To be clear, use **würde**: *Wenn ich weniger arbeiten **würde**, …*","examples":[{"de":"Wenn er mehr **lernen würde**, **hätte** er bessere Noten.","en":"If he studied more, he'd get better marks."}],"source":"synkretismus"},{"title":"Some one-word forms you'll read and hear","text":"Strong verbs also have one-word forms. Most sound formal (*käme, ginge*), but a few are common in speech too: *es **gäbe*** (there would be), *ich **wüsste*** (I would know), *ich **bräuchte*** (I would need).","examples":[{"de":"**Wüssten** Sie, wo das Sitzungszimmer ist?","en":"Would you know where the meeting room is?"},{"de":"Es **wäre** gut, wenn es mehr Parkplätze **gäbe**.","en":"It would be good if there were more parking spaces."}],"source":"flexion"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Ich würde gern einen Kaffee haben.","right":"Ich **hätte** gern einen Kaffee.","why":"With *haben* and *sein*, use *hätte* and *wäre*, not *würde … haben / sein*. The *würde* version isn't wrong, but it sounds clumsy."},{"wrong":"Wenn ich Zeit hätte, ich würde kommen.","right":"Wenn ich Zeit hätte, **würde ich** kommen.","why":"After a *wenn*-clause, the main clause starts with the verb."},{"wrong":"Wenn ich würde Zeit haben, …","right":"Wenn ich Zeit **hätte**, …","why":"In the *wenn*-clause the verb goes to the end, and *haben* uses *hätte*."},{"wrong":"Ich könnte gestern nicht kommen.","right":"Ich **konnte** gestern nicht kommen.","why":"*konnte* (without umlaut) = was able to. *könnte* = could, would be able to. The umlaut changes the meaning."},{"wrong":"Wenn ich das gewusst würde, …","right":"Wenn ich das gewusst **hätte**, …","why":"For the past, use *hätte* or *wäre* plus the Partizip II."}],"examples":[{"de":"Ich **hätte** gern einen Termin am Dienstag.","en":"I'd like an appointment on Tuesday."},{"de":"**Könntest** du mir kurz helfen?","en":"Could you help me for a moment?"},{"de":"Es **wäre** schön, wenn wir uns bald **sehen würden**.","en":"It would be nice if we saw each other soon."},{"de":"An deiner Stelle **würde** ich das Angebot **annehmen**.","en":"If I were you, I'd accept the offer."},{"de":"Ich **wäre** lieber Lehrerin geworden.","en":"I would rather have become a teacher."},{"de":"Wenn ich in Zürich **wohnen würde**, **müsste** ich nicht so lange pendeln.","en":"If I lived in Zurich, I wouldn't have to commute so far."},{"de":"**Hätten** Sie morgen Zeit für ein kurzes Gespräch?","en":"Would you have time for a short talk tomorrow?"},{"de":"Wir **sollten** früher losfahren.","en":"We should leave earlier."}],"later":"**Konjunktiv I** (*er sei, sie habe*) is used for reported speech, mainly in the news and formal texts. You only need to recognise it later, at B2.","selfcheck":{"types":{"fill":{"label":"Fill the gap","instruction":"Put the verb in brackets into the Konjunktiv II. Write one word.","example":{"q":"Ich ___ gern Urlaub. (haben)","a":"hätte","why":"*haben* → *hätte*, one word, no *würde*."}},"polite":{"label":"Make it polite","instruction":"Turn the direct command into a polite question with *Könnten Sie* or *Würden Sie*. Write the whole sentence. *bitte* is optional.","example":{"q":"Schicken Sie mir den Plan.","a":"Könnten Sie mir bitte den Plan schicken?","why":"*Könnten* comes first, and the infinitive goes to the end."}}},"items":[{"type":"fill","q":"Wenn ich du ___, würde ich das Angebot annehmen. (sein)","a":["wäre"],"why":"*sein* → *wäre*. In the *wenn*-clause the verb goes to the end."},{"type":"fill","q":"___ Sie mir bitte helfen? (können)","a":["Könnten"],"why":"Polite question: *Könnten Sie …?*"},{"type":"fill","q":"Du ___ zum Arzt gehen. (sollen)","a":["solltest"],"why":"Advice with *sollen*: *du solltest*, with no umlaut."},{"type":"fill","q":"Wir ___ gern ein grösseres Büro. (haben)","a":["hätten"],"why":"*wir hätten*, with the *-en* ending for *wir*."},{"type":"fill","q":"Wenn ich früher aufgestanden ___, hätte ich den Bus erreicht. (sein)","a":["wäre"],"why":"Past: *aufstehen* forms its Perfekt with *sein*, so *wäre aufgestanden*."},{"type":"polite","q":"Geben Sie mir das Formular.","a":["Könnten Sie mir das Formular geben?","Könnten Sie mir bitte das Formular geben?","Könnten Sie mir das Formular bitte geben?","Würden Sie mir das Formular geben?","Würden Sie mir bitte das Formular geben?","Würden Sie mir das Formular bitte geben?"],"why":"*Könnten Sie* or *Würden Sie* first, with *geben* at the end."}]},"sources":[{"id":"k2","title":"grammis: Konjunktiv II","url":"https://grammis.ids-mannheim.de/terminologie/401"},{"id":"flexion","title":"grammis: Verbflexion nach Tempus und Modus (verb forms by tense and mood)","url":"https://grammis.ids-mannheim.de/progr@mm/4047"},{"id":"modal","title":"grammis: Modalverb (modal verbs)","url":"https://grammis.ids-mannheim.de/progr@mm/5202"},{"id":"synkretismus","title":"grammis: Formensynkretismus von Konjunktiv und Indikativ (when subjunctive and indicative look the same)","url":"https://grammis.ids-mannheim.de/systematische-grammatik/315"}]}];
